.\" $Revision: 1.3 $
.\" $Log: getpass.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:44  dlr
.\" Add a verified $HEX[] recovery example. Shows a wrapped colon-bearing password passing through unchanged without -u and decoding to pass:word with it. Adds the case that matters in practice: when feeding results back as a wordlist the wrapper should usually be KEPT, because mdxfind decodes $HEX[] on input, so leaving it alone round-trips a password that -u would render unparseable. Verified both directions by running.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:00  dlr
.\" Remove the redundant .PP immediately after .SH EXAMPLES. mandoc reports it as "skipping paragraph macro: PP after SH"; groff does not flag it at all, which is how it survived a groff-only check. macOS and the BSDs render with mandoc, so mandoc is what most readers of these pages actually see. No rendered output changes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: getpass(1) man page. Default trailing-field extraction, the .txt basename expansion and the built-in excluded-extension list, all 10 options, and a note distinguishing it from getpass(3).
.\"
.TH GETPASS 1 "2026-08-29" "getpass 1.11" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
getpass \- extract plaintexts from hash result files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B getpass
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getpass
pulls the plaintext out of
.BR mdxfind (1)
result files and writes it to standard output.  With no
.I file
argument it reads standard input.
.PP
By default it takes everything after the
.I last
delimiter on each line, which is the password field in both the
.IB hash : password
and
.IB hash : salt : password
result shapes.  Lines with no delimiter are dropped.
.SH "FILENAME EXPANSION"
.B getpass
understands the file-naming convention that
.BR mdsplit (1)
maintains.  Naming a
.B .txt
file expands it to every file sharing that basename, so
.PP
.RS
.nf
getpass 50m.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
harvests the plaintexts from
.BR 50m.MD5x01 ,
.BR 50m.SHA1x02 ,
and every other solution file belonging to the 50m list, without touching
.B 50m.txt
itself.
.PP
This works because
.B getpass
carries a list of excluded extensions \(em by default
.BR .txt ,
.BR .orig ,
.BR .test ,
.BR .csalt.txt ,
.B .fixme
and
.B .new
\(em which name files that hold unsolved hashes or source material rather
than results.  Files matching an excluded extension are skipped, with a
notice on standard error.
.PP
Use
.B \-t
to disable the expansion and
.B \-n
to disable the exclusion list.  Both together make
.B getpass
process exactly the files named.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " field"
Extract field
.IR field ,
counting from 1, instead of the trailing field.
.TP
.BI \-c " colspec"
Extract by column, in the style of
.BR cut (1):
.IR N \-,
.IR N \- M ,
or
.RI \- M .
Columns count from 1.
.TP
.BI \-d " delim"
Set the field delimiter.  Accepts a literal character, a decimal value, or
a
.BI 0x NN
hexadecimal value.  Default
.BR : .
.TP
.B \-u
Decode
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
wrappers on output.
.TP
.B \-t
Disable
.B .txt
extension expansion.
.TP
.B \-n
Disable the excluded-extension list entirely.  Equivalent to
.BR "\-x /dev/null" .
.TP
.BI \-x " file"
Read the excluded-extension list from
.IR file ,
one extension per line, replacing the built-in list.
.TP
.BI \-S " spec"
Mark characters as requiring
.B $HEX[]
encoding on output.
.I spec
is a comma-separated list of literal characters, decimal values,
.BI 0x NN
hexadecimal values, and ranges such as
.BR a\-f ,
.B 0\-32
or
.BR 0x1a\-0x1f .
The resulting 256-entry map is printed to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-U " spec"
As
.BR \-S ,
but unmarks.
.B "\-U 0\-255"
disables
.B $HEX[]
encoding completely.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary, including the default exclusion list, and exit.
.SH NOTES
This is an unrelated program to the
.BR getpass (3)
C library function.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error or an unreadable exclude file.
Unreadable input files are reported and skipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Collect every plaintext recovered from a list, ready to feed back as a
wordlist:
.PP
.RS
.nf
getpass 50m.txt | rling stdin found.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Recover a password that
.BR mdxfind (1)
had to wrap because it contains a colon.  Without
.BR \-u ,
the wrapper is passed through as written:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cat res.MD5x01
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]

$ getpass \-t \-n res.MD5x01
$HEX[706173733a776f7264]

$ getpass \-t \-n \-u res.MD5x01
pass:word
.fi
.RE
.IP
Feeding results back as a wordlist is the one case where the wrapper should
usually be
.I kept:
.BR mdxfind (1)
decodes
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
on input, so leaving it alone round-trips a password that
.B \-u
would render unparseable.
.PP
Pull the second colon-separated field out of an arbitrary file, ignoring
the naming convention entirely:
.PP
.RS
.nf
getpass \-t \-n \-f 2 arbitrary.csv
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR mdsplit (1),
.BR rling (1),
.BR rehex (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
